The following setup of the analysis framework and application under test was used:
• only data flow originating in input files and command line arguments was processed during trace generation;
• initial input files and command line arguments were constructed manually after a brief stage of inspecting the application process and usage directions;
• DynamoRIO-based tracer and checker plugins were used;
• Z3 was employed as a solver to process SMT queries and generate new input data;
• SMT queries were generated with the necessary directives to employ incremental solving;
• analysis time was limited to 6 hours for every application;
• analysis was performed in multithreaded mode with 5 active CPU cores;
Every application was analyzed through two independent sessions: the first session allowed the analysis framework to focus solely on command line arguments.

Dynamically detecting performance bottlenecks 
for program optimization has been demonstrated to be effective [Luk et al. 2005; Bruening et al. 2003; 
Lu et al. 2003; Sullivan et al. 2003; Lu et al. 2005; Lu et al. 2004] for performance enhancing.

Previous work has researched both trace selection [Duesterwald and Bala 2000; 
Hiniker et al. 2005; Hiser et al. 2006] and linking strategies [Bala et al. 2000; Bruening et al. 2003; 
Desoli et al. 2002; ke Chen et al. 2000; Luk et al. 2005].
